<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Suez University - Bone Histology Exam</title>
    <style>
        /* الإعدادات الهيكلية المتطابقة تماماً مع الأوراق الرسمية لكلية الطب جامعة السويس */
        body {
            font-family: 'Helvetica Neue', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
            color: #334155;
            background-color: #f1f5f9;
            margin: 0;
            padding: 0;
            line-height: 1.6;
            -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact;
            print-color-adjust: exact;
        }
        
        /* حاوية الصفحة القياسية A4 لضمان ثبات الأبعاد عند التحويل إلى PDF */
        .page {
            background-color: #ffffff;
            width: 210mm;
            min-height: 297mm;
            padding: 20mm;
            margin: 15px auto;
            box-sizing: border-box;
            box-shadow: 0 4px 10px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: column;
            position: relative;
        }

        /* الهيدر الأزرق الداكن الأساسي للمحاضرة */
        .header-blue {
            background-color: #003366;
            color: #ffffff;
            padding: 22px 20px;
            border-radius: 4px;
            text-align: left;
            margin-bottom: 25px;
        }
        .header-blue h1 {
            margin: 0;
            font-size: 24px;
            font-weight: 700;
            text-transform: uppercase;
            letter-spacing: 0.5px;
        }
        .header-blue .subtitle {
            font-size: 13px;
            margin-top: 6px;
            color: #93c5fd;
            font-weight: 500;
        }

        /* علامات الأقسام الجانبية */
        .section-title {
            border-left: 4px solid #003366;
            padding-left: 10px;
            color: #003366;
            font-weight: 700;
            font-size: 14px;
            margin: 20px 0 15px 0;
            text-transform: uppercase;
            letter-spacing: 0.5px;
        }

        /* مستطيل حول كل سؤال بشكل مستقل لحمايته من الانقسام */
        .question-box {
            border: 1px solid #e2e8f0;
            border-radius: 6px;
            padding: 16px;
            margin-bottom: 14px;
            background-color: #ffffff;
            page-break-inside: avoid;
        }

        .question-text {
            font-weight: 600;
            font-size: 14.5px;
            color: #0f172a;
            margin-bottom: 12px;
        }

        .options-container {
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: column;
            gap: 6px;
            padding-left: 5px;
        }

        .option-item {
            font-size: 13.5px;
            color: #475569;
            padding: 2px 0;
        }
        
        .option-item strong {
            color: #0f172a;
            margin-right: 4px;
        }

        /* شريط الإجابات الفوري الذكي أسفل كل صفحة مستقلة */
        .footer-wrapper {
            margin-top: auto;
            padding-top: 20px;
        }
        
        .key-banner {
            background-color: #e6f0fa;
            border-left: 4px solid #003366;
            padding: 10px 16px;
            border-radius: 4px;
            font-size: 13px;
            font-weight: 700;
            color: #003366;
            margin-bottom: 12px;
            letter-spacing: 0.3px;
        }

        .footer-meta {
            display: flex;
            justify-content: space-between;
            font-size: 11px;
            color: #94a3b8;
            border-top: 1px solid #f1f5f9;
            padding-top: 8px;
            font-weight: 600;
        }

        @media print {
            body { 
                background-color: #ffffff; 
                padding: 0; 
            }
            .page {
                margin: 0;
                border: none;
                box-shadow: none;
                page-break-after: always;
                width: 100%;
                min-height: 100vh;
            }
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <!-- PAGE 1 -->
    <div class="page">
        <div class="header-blue">
            <h1>Histology of Bone Tissue - Comprehensive Bank</h1>
            <div class="subtitle">Suez University - Faculty of Medicine | Examination Version</div>
        </div>

        <div class="section-title">Part 1: General Characters, Growth & Periosteal Coverings</div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q1. Histologically, how is bone tissue distinct from cartilage tissue regarding its blood supply and physical consistency?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Bone is avascular and rigid, while cartilage is highly vascular and hard.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Both tissues are completely avascular but bone has a harder calcified matrix.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Bone is highly vascular and hard, while cartilage is avascular and rigid.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Bone is avascular and hard, while cartilage is highly vascular and rigid.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q2. During bone remodeling and development, appositional growth is responsible for increasing which of the following skeletal dimensions?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Bone width</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Bone length</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Total number of osteons</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Medullary cavity length</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q3. A researcher examines a section of the outer fibrous layer of the periosteum. Which group of histological components should be most abundant?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Osteogenic cells, osteoblasts, and elastic fibers</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Single layer of flattened cells with minimal extracellular matrix</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Loose connective tissue entirely lacking blood vessels</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Dense collagen fibers, fibroblasts, and many blood vessels</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q4. Which statement perfectly describes the nature and role of Sharpey's fibers in bone histology?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> They are type II collagen fibers secreted by chondrocytes to line the endosteum.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> They are penetrating type I collagen fibers from the outer periosteum anchoring it to the bone matrix.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> They are elastic fibers running exclusively inside the central Haversian canals.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> They are specialized cytoplasmic extensions linking osteoclasts together.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q5. Histologically, the endosteum is characterized as:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> A thick, calcified outer protective layer containing dense fibrous networks.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> A multi-layered structure of active multinucleated giant cells.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> A single layer of flattened osteogenic cells and osteoblasts lining internal cavities.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> An avascular sheath covering the articular ends of long bones.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q6. The organic component of the bone matrix (osteoid) constitutes approximately 35% of its dry weight. Which molecule forms more than 90% of this organic framework?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Collagen fibers type I</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Collagen fibers type II</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Chondroitin and keratan sulphate</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Osteocalcin and osteopontin</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-wrapper">
            <div class="key-banner">📍 PAGE 1 ANSWER KEY: Q1: C | Q2: A | Q3: D | Q4: B | Q5: C | Q6: A</div>
            <div class="footer-meta">
                <span>SU-MED | Locomotor Module</span>
                <span>Page 1 of 6</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- PAGE 2 -->
    <div class="page">
        <div class="header-blue">
            <h1>Histology of Bone Tissue - Comprehensive Bank</h1>
            <div class="subtitle">Suez University - Faculty of Medicine | Examination Version</div>
        </div>

        <div class="section-title">Part 2: Bone Matrix & Bone Cells I (Osteogenic & Osteoblasts)</div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q7. Which inorganic mineral combination is primarily responsible for the characteristic hardness and rigidity of the bone matrix, making up 65% of its dry weight?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Bicarbonate and Citrate</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Sodium and Potassium</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Magnesium and Proteoglycans</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Calcium and Phosphorus</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q8. Which of the following is the ONLY type of bone cell that retains the biological capacity to undergo mitotic cell division?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Osteoblast</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Osteogenic cell</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Osteocyte</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Osteoclast</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q9. Embryonically, osteogenic cells directly develop from which specific cell lineage?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Hematopoietic stem cells</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Monocyte precursor cells</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Undifferentiated mesenchymal cells (UMCs)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Fully differentiated chondroblasts</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q10. Under the light microscope, active bone-forming osteoblasts display a deeply basophilic cytoplasm and PAS-positive granules. This histochemical characteristic is correlated with their rich production of:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Alkaline phosphatase</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Acid phosphatase</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Collagenase</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Carbonic anhydrase</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q11. Active osteoblasts are structurally interconnected with neighboring cells on the bone surface via processes containing which type of intercellular junction?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Desmosomes</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Tight junctions</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Zonula occludens</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Gap junctions</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q12. In addition to synthesizing type I collagen and bone proteins, osteoblasts produce multiple hematopoietic cytokines. Which of the following is NOT synthesized by osteoblasts?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Granulocyte-colony stimulating factor (G-CSF)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Acid phosphatase lysosomal packets</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Macrophage-colony stimulating factor (M-CSF)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Interleukins</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-wrapper">
            <div class="key-banner">📍 PAGE 2 ANSWER KEY: Q7: D | Q8: B | Q9: C | Q10: A | Q11: D | Q12: B</div>
            <div class="footer-meta">
                <span>SU-MED | Locomotor Module</span>
                <span>Page 2 of 6</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- PAGE 3 -->
    <div class="page">
        <div class="header-blue">
            <h1>Histology of Bone Tissue - Comprehensive Bank</h1>
            <div class="subtitle">Suez University - Faculty of Medicine | Examination Version</div>
        </div>

        <div class="section-title">Part 3: Bone Cells II (Osteocytes & Osteoclasts)</div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q13. When an active osteoblast becomes completely surrounded by its newly synthesized mineralized matrix, it transforms into which mature cell type?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Osteogenic stem cell</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Multinucleated osteoclast</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Mature osteocyte</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Fibroblast</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q14. In contrast to chondrocytes in cartilage, mature osteocytes residing within the bone matrix:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Present singly inside individual lacunae and cannot divide to form cell nests.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Form large isogenous cell nests inside irregular lacunae.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Retain a high mitotic rate resulting in rapid interstitial bone growth.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Completely lack dendritic cytoplasmic processes.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q15. Through which microscopic channels do the dendritic processes of osteocytes extend to communicate via gap junctions and receive nutrients?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Volkmann's canals</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Haversian canals</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Howship's lacunae</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Canaliculi</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q16. Osteoclasts are specialized motile bone-resorbing cells. From which cellular lineage do functional osteoclasts originate?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Mitotic division of inner osteoblasts</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Bone marrow precursors in common with monocytes</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Undifferentiated mesenchymal cells lining the periosteum</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Direct transformation of aging osteocytes</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q17. Under the light microscope, osteoclasts are easily recognized because they are:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Large, irregular multinucleated giant cells with acidophilic cytoplasm sitting in Howship's lacunae.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Small, elongated spindle-shaped cells with pale basophilic cytoplasm.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Cuboidal, single-nucleated cells arranged side-by-side on growing bone surfaces.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Mononucleated cells exhibiting intense cytoplasmic PAS positivity and no processes.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q18. Lysosomes are highly abundant in the osteoclast cytoplasm, imparting strong histochemical positivity for which specific enzyme marker?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Alkaline phosphatase</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Carbonic anhydrase</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Acid phosphatase</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Hyaluronidase</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-wrapper">
            <div class="key-banner">📍 PAGE 3 ANSWER KEY: Q13: C | Q14: A | Q15: D | Q16: B | Q17: A | Q18: C</div>
            <div class="footer-meta">
                <span>SU-MED | Locomotor Module</span>
                <span>Page 3 of 6</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- PAGE 4 -->
    <div class="page">
        <div class="header-blue">
            <h1>Histology of Bone Tissue - Comprehensive Bank</h1>
            <div class="subtitle">Suez University - Faculty of Medicine | Examination Version</div>
        </div>

        <div class="section-title">Part 4: Ultrastructure, Histological Techniques & Tissue Maturity</div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q19. Under Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM), the osteoclast is divided into 4 zones. Which zone lacks major organelles, contains actin microfilaments, and acts as the sealing zone against the bone matrix?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Ruffled border</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Vesicular zone</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Basal zone</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Clear zone</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q20. During bone resorption, how do osteoclasts achieve the dissolution and decalcification of the inorganic calcium salts within the matrix?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> By secreting alkaline phosphatase inside the compartments</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> By secreting excess H+ ions to produce an acidic microenvironment</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> By producing large quantities of type II collagen fibrils</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> By executing phagocytosis of entire intact osteons</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q21. To prepare a bone section for standard histological staining (H&E), a specimen is placed in a nitric acid solution or EDTA. What is the fundamental mechanism and result of this decalcified bone technique?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> It removes calcium salts to render the bone soft enough for routine sectioning.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> It destroys all organic components leaving only hard inorganic minerals.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> It prevents air from entering the lacunae so cells appear completely black.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> It preserves the exact mineralization rate without altering cell morphology.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q22. During the microscopic examination of a ground bone section prepared by sawing dry bone into thin slices without staining, the lacunae appear as sharp black spaces. What is the cause of this appearance?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Silver nitrate dyes precipitate selectively within active osteocyte nuclei.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Active osteoblasts fill the spaces with dark, calcified mineral pigments.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> The cells are destroyed during grinding, leaving air-filled spaces that scatter light.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Intact osteoclasts accumulate iron and potassium inside their compartments.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q23. Which of the following is a primary feature distinguishing primary (immature or woven) bone from secondary (mature or lamellar) bone?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Primary bone contains significantly more mineral content than secondary bone.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Primary bone exhibits an irregular or random arrangement of collagen fibers.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Primary bone is organized into fully developed Haversian systems.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Primary bone never appears during the healing of adult bone fractures.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q24. In adults, primary (immature) bone tissue is normally replaced by mature bone. In which specific anatomical area does primary bone persist permanently?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Shaft of long bones</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Outer and inner tables of flat skull bones</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Diploë layer of the sternum</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Tooth alveoli and insertion sites of some tendons</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-wrapper">
            <div class="key-banner">📍 PAGE 4 ANSWER KEY: Q19: D | Q20: B | Q21: A | Q22: C | Q23: B | Q24: D</div>
            <div class="footer-meta">
                <span>SU-MED | Locomotor Module</span>
                <span>Page 4 of 6</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- PAGE 5 -->
    <div class="page">
        <div class="header-blue">
            <h1>Histology of Bone Tissue - Comprehensive Bank</h1>
            <div class="subtitle">Suez University - Faculty of Medicine | Examination Version</div>
        </div>

        <div class="section-title">Part 5: Architectural Classification & Lamellar Systems</div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q25. Where is compact cortical bone tissue normally found within the human skeletal system?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Shafts (diaphyses) of long bones and inner/outer tables of flat skull bones.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Epiphyses (ends) of long bones exclusively.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Deep interior core of short and irregular bones only.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Lining the central core of all embryonic bone trabeculae.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q26. What is the term used for the thin layer of spongy bone enclosed between the inner and outer tables of compact bone in the skull?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Cement line</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Osteon core</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Diploë</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Trabecular matrix</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q27. The basic structural and functional repeating unit of mature compact bone is the Haversian system (osteon). Each fully developed osteon consists of:</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Thin interconnected rods called trabeculae with large irregular marrow spaces.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> A cylinder of 4 to 20 concentric bone lamellae arranged around a central canal.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Parallel arrays of non-calcified type II collagen fibers without cells.</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> A single large multi-nucleated osteoclast enclosing a network of canaliculi.</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q28. What does the central Haversian canal of an osteon contain histologically?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Fluid-filled cartilage matrix completely lacking cells or nerves</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Pure dense irregular connective tissue without any blood vessels</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Highly calcified crystalline plates of magnetic hydroxyapatite</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Loose connective tissue rich in blood vessels, nerves, and an osteogenic cell lining</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q29. A student observes vascular channels running perpendicular or obliquely to the long axis of the bone, connecting Haversian canals to each other and to the periosteum. These channels are not surrounded by concentric lamellae. What are they?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Volkmann's canals</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Canaliculi</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Central canals</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Howship's pathways</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">Q30. Which system represents the angular fragments of old, partially resorbed osteons located between fully intact Haversian systems in compact bone?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Outer circumferential lamellae</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Inner circumferential lamellae</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Interstitial lamellae</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Trabecular lamellae</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-wrapper">
